    "bottomLine": "If you want one 18V drill that will handle hole saws, spade bits and long lag pilots without complaint, this is the one to buy.",
    "description": "The 2903-20 is Milwaukee's current full-size M18 FUEL drill/driver, and it is the drill we would hand to anyone who asks for one tool that will not run out of muscle. Milwaukee rates it at 1,400 in-lbs of torque with a two-speed gearbox topping out at 2,100 RPM, which puts it at the top of this list on paper. Remember that the torque number is Milwaukee's own rating and is not measured the same way as the figures other brands publish, so treat it as a ranking within Milwaukee's line rather than a head-to-head score. What matters in practice is that this class of drill turns 2-1/2-inch hole saws, large spade bits and auger bits through framing lumber without bogging, and the clutch keeps it civil for cabinet screws. The 1/2-inch all-metal chuck with carbide teeth holds round-shank bits firmly, and the 6.9-inch length is short for a full-power drill. The catch is cost of entry: the 2903-20 is sold as a bare tool, so a first-time buyer needs a battery and charger, and the kit versions change by retailer. If you already own M18 batteries, it is the easiest recommendation on this page. If you do not, read our Milwaukee vs DeWalt comparison before you commit to a platform."
