      "Small-battery kits trade runtime for weight",
      "Not a hammer drill; masonry work needs the DCD805 instead"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right drill for most homeowners: small, light, plenty strong, and on a platform with a tool for almost every job.",
    "description": "The DCD800 is DeWalt's current compact XR drill/driver and the pick we would point most homeowners toward. DeWalt lists the head at 6.37 inches long and the bare tool at 2.82 lb, so it is easy to hold at arm's length while you drive screws into a ceiling joist or a cabinet back. Speed runs to 2,000 RPM in high gear and 650 RPM in low. DeWalt does not publish an in-lbs torque figure for it; it rates power in Unit Watts Out and says the DCD800 delivers up to 40 percent more UWO than the older DCD791 when paired with a 5.0Ah or POWERSTACK battery. That is a useful way to compare DeWalt drills with each other, but it cannot be lined up against Milwaukee or Makita torque numbers. In use, owners commonly report it handles deck screws, 1-inch spade bits and small hole saws with ease, and it only feels outclassed when you lean on large self-feed bits. It is sold bare as the DCD800B and in several kits with different batteries, so compare what is in the box before buying. If you need to drill brick or concrete, the hammer-drill version, the DCD805, shares the same body."
