    ],
    "bottomLine": "A refined, ready-to-go kit for homeowners and remodelers who value balance and control over raw power.",
    "description": "The XFD131 is the kit to buy if you want to open one box and be done. It pairs Makita's compact brushless 1/2-inch driver-drill with an 18V LXT 3.0Ah battery and charger, a battery size that keeps the tool light while holding enough charge for a day of household projects. Makita lists 440 in-lbs of max torque and two speeds of 0-500 and 0-1,900 RPM, with an overall length of 6-5/8 inches and 3.8 lb with the battery fitted. Those numbers put it behind the full-size 18V drills on raw output, and Makita measures torque its own way, so the gap is not a precise figure either. What the XFD131 does well is feel. Owners consistently describe the trigger as smooth and progressive and the clutch as predictable, which is what you want when driving brass hinge screws or assembling flat-pack furniture without stripping heads. It handles typical home drilling in wood and light steel comfortably. If you already own LXT batteries, look for the bare-tool version instead. If you need masonry capability, step up to the XPH14 hammer driver-drill further down this list."
  },
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-3403-20",
    "name": "Milwaukee M12 FUEL 3403-20 1/2 in. Drill/Driver",
    "category": "12V brushless subcompact drill/driver, bare tool",
    "badge": "Best Compact 12V",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Max Torque (Milwaukee rating)",
        "400 in-lbs"
