    "bottomLine": "The best small drill for electricians, cabinet installers and anyone who works in tight spaces.",
    "description": "The M12 FUEL 3403-20 is the drill to get if size matters more than headline power. Milwaukee lists it at 5.9 inches long and 2.5 lb, and rates it at 400 in-lbs of torque, which is more than several compact 18V drills claim. The two-speed gearbox runs 0-450 and 0-1,550 RPM, and it keeps a full 1/2-inch all-metal chuck rather than the 3/8-inch chuck many 12V drills use, so it accepts the same large-shank bits as its bigger siblings. The trade-off is speed and stamina. A 1,550 RPM top speed makes it slower through wood with spade bits than the 2,000 RPM-plus 18V drills, and a small M12 battery will not last as long on hole saws. For the work it is built for, though, it is excellent: drilling pilot holes inside a cabinet, running screws overhead, installing hardware, and working from a ladder where every ounce matters. Many tradespeople carry this drill alongside an M12 impact driver as an everyday pair and leave the M18 tools in the truck. If you already own M12 tools, buy the bare 3403-20. If not, look for a kit that includes two batteries."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xph14",
    "name": "Makita XPH14 18V LXT Brushless 1/2 in. Hammer Driver-Drill",
    "category": "18V LXT brushless hammer driver-drill",
    "badge": "Best Hammer Drill",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "specs": [
