        "7 in."
      ],
      [
        "Weight (with battery)",
        "6 lb"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "Hammer mode up to 31,500 BPM for brick, block and occasional concrete",
      "Makita rates it at 1,250 in-lbs, its highest-torque 18V drill",
      "7-inch length is short for a full-size hammer drill",
      "Self-ratcheting all-metal chuck",
      "Doubles as a heavy-duty drill/driver when hammer mode is off"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Makita lists 6 lb with a battery, heavy for overhead driving",
      "Overkill if you never drill masonry",
      "Not a substitute for an SDS rotary hammer on regular concrete work"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it if you drill into brick or block a few times a year and want one drill that can do everything else too.",
    "description": "A hammer drill adds a percussion action that chips at masonry while the bit spins, and the XPH14 is our pick for anyone who needs that occasionally. Makita rates it at 1,250 in-lbs of torque, with two speeds of 0-550 and 0-2,100 RPM and a hammer rate of up to 31,500 blows per minute in high gear. The all-metal self-ratcheting 1/2-inch chuck grips carbide masonry bits firmly, and the head is 7 inches long. The cost is weight: Makita lists the tool at 6 lb with a battery installed, which you will notice after an hour of overhead screws. That is why we rank it as the hammer pick rather than the everyday drill. For anchoring a TV mount into brick, drilling block for a mailbox post bracket, or running Tapcon pilots into a basement wall, a hammer drill like this is the right tool. For drilling dozens of holes into poured concrete, rent or buy an SDS-plus rotary hammer instead; no cordless hammer drill is efficient at that. Switch the hammer off and the XPH14 is also a powerful conventional drill, so it can be the only drill in a garage that sees mixed work."
