        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people the best cordless drill is a compact brushless 18V or 20V drill/driver from a battery platform you are happy to keep buying into. Our overall pick is the Milwaukee M18 FUEL 2903-20, because it has the most headroom of any drill here and sits on one of the two deepest professional platforms. For a homeowner who mostly hangs shelves, builds furniture and does small repairs, the lighter DeWalt DCD800 is the better day-to-day tool, and it is the drill we would buy for a first apartment or house. If budget leads, the Ryobi PBLDD01 gives you a brushless motor and a big tool family for noticeably less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two other picks earn a place for specific jobs. The Milwaukee M12 FUEL 3403-20 is the drill for tight spaces and long days on a ladder, and the Makita XPH14 adds a hammer mode for brick and block. The Makita XFD131 kit and the Bosch GSR18V-535CN round out the list for buyers who already own those batteries or want a complete, balanced kit in one box.
          </p>

          <p>
            We have not run these drills through our own bench tests. The specs on this page come from each maker's published figures, checked against retailer spec tables where the maker's page was incomplete, and the judgments come from how each class of drill behaves in normal use and what owners consistently report. Where a number is a manufacturer's rating, we say so, because the most quoted number on a drill box, torque, is also the least comparable.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more thing before the picks. The drill you choose is really a choice of battery. Batteries cost as much as the tool, they only fit one brand, and once you own two of them every other tool you buy will follow. Our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> guide goes through that decision in depth. If you are starting from zero, read it first, then come back and pick the drill on that platform from the list below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drill-or-driver" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Drill/Driver, Hammer Drill or Impact Driver?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three tools get called a cordless drill, and they are not interchangeable. A drill/driver has a three-jaw chuck that grips round or hex bits, a clutch that slips at a set torque so you do not sink screws too deep, and usually two gear ranges. It is the general-purpose tool: drilling holes in wood, metal and plastic, and driving small to medium screws with control. Every pick on this page is a drill/driver at heart.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hammer drill is a drill/driver with an extra mode that adds a rapid forward percussion to the rotation. That action chips at brick, block and mortar so a carbide masonry bit can make progress. With hammer mode off it works like a normal drill, which is why a hammer drill can be your only drill if you occasionally anchor into masonry. The trade-off is a slightly longer, heavier body. The Makita XPH14 is our pick here, and DeWalt sells the DCD805 as the hammer twin of the DCD800.
          </p>

          <p>
            An impact driver is a different tool. It has a quick-release 1/4-inch hex collet instead of a chuck, no clutch, and an internal hammer and anvil that deliver rotational blows when the fastener resists. That makes it far better than a drill for driving long structural screws, lag bolts and hundreds of deck screws, and far worse at drilling precise holes. We cover those in a separate guide to the <a href="/best-impact-driver/">best impact driver</a>, and most people who do any building end up owning both.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can only buy one tool, buy the drill/driver. It can drill and drive, while an impact driver can only drive well. If you are buying two, a drill and impact combo kit on one battery platform is usually the best value in cordless tools, and both of our guides are organized so you can pick a matching pair.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drill for holes, impact for long screws.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A drill/driver with a clutch is the precise tool for drilling and small fasteners. An impact driver is the brute for long screws and lags. Most households need the drill first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

