        <div id="specs-that-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Specs Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drill boxes are covered in numbers, and only some of them predict how a drill will feel on the job. Here is how to read them, in order of usefulness.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Torque ratings are not comparable across brands</h3>

          <p>
            Torque is the twisting force a drill can deliver, and it is the number that decides whether a big bit stalls. The trouble is that there is no single industry test behind the figures on the box. Milwaukee, Makita, Ryobi and Bosch publish in-lbs figures measured by their own internal methods, which can differ in whether the number is a peak or a sustained value, what battery is fitted, and how the load is applied. DeWalt mostly publishes Unit Watts Out, a power figure, rather than torque. So a 750 in-lbs Ryobi rating does not prove that drill out-twists a DeWalt with no published figure, and a 1,400 in-lbs Milwaukee rating is not exactly twice a 700 in-lbs rating from another brand. Use torque to compare drills from the same maker, where the method is consistent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Speed and gearing</h3>

          <p>
            Top speed in high gear tells you how quickly a drill will bore small holes in wood and metal. Most full-size drills land around 2,000 RPM, and that is plenty. Low gear, usually 450 to 650 RPM, is where large bits and screws live, because it multiplies torque. Two gears are standard on every pick here; you do not need three.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chuck, clutch, size and weight</h3>

          <p>
            A 1/2-inch chuck is the norm and accepts larger shanks than a 3/8-inch chuck. All-metal ratcheting chucks hold bits better than plastic sleeves. The clutch, the numbered collar behind the chuck, is what lets you drive a screw to the same depth every time; more positions give finer steps, which is why the Ryobi's 24-position clutch is a real plus for furniture work. Head length and weight decide comfort. A drill that is 2.5 lb bare will feel very different from one that is 6 lb with a battery after an hour overhead, so check which figure a maker quotes, since some list bare weight and others list weight with a battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brushless motors</h3>

          <p>
            Brushless motors run cooler and get more work out of each battery charge than brushed motors, and they are now standard at every price point that matters. Every drill on this page is brushless. If a bargain drill is still brushed, it is usually a sign the rest of the tool is old too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Buy the Battery Platform, Not the Drill
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the expensive truth about cordless tools: the drill is the cheap part. Two good batteries and a charger often cost as much as the drill itself, and they only fit one brand. Once you own them, every future purchase, from a circular saw to a leaf blower, will pull toward the same color. That is why we rank platforms before drills.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milwaukee M18 and DeWalt 20V MAX are the two deepest professional platforms in North America, with hundreds of tools each and wide availability at hardware stores and online. Our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> comparison covers batteries, warranty, tool range and service in detail, and for most buyers either is a safe, long-term choice. If you lean toward small, light tools for interior work, Milwaukee's M12 line adds a second, compact family that DeWalt does not match as closely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi ONE+ is the homeowner platform. Its tools are cheaper, its range is very broad, including many outdoor and household gadgets, and its batteries have stayed compatible for decades. The trade-off is that its pro-grade tools are fewer, and some heavy tools feel built to a price. Our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a> goes through where the platform is worth it and where it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makita LXT and Bosch 18V are excellent platforms with smaller followings in the US. Makita's range is enormous and especially strong in outdoor power equipment; Bosch is strongest in saws, routers and measuring tools. Both are fine choices, especially if you already own a battery or two. What you should avoid is buying a cheap drill on an orphan platform, or splitting your first purchases across three brands. Pick one, buy the drill, and let it anchor the rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical tip: when a drill and impact driver combo kit on your chosen platform costs only a little more than the drill kit alone, buy the combo. It is almost always cheaper than adding the impact driver later, and our <a href="/best-impact-driver/">best impact driver</a> guide can help you check that the included driver is a current model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Bare Tool or Kit: What Is in the Box
          </h2>

          <p>
            Model numbers tell you what you are buying, and misreading them is the most common reason a drill arrives without a battery. Milwaukee uses a -20 suffix for the bare tool, so the 2903-20 and 3403-20 come with no battery or charger. DeWalt uses a B suffix for bare tools, as in DCD800B. Makita uses Z for bare tools, so XPH14Z is the hammer drill alone, while kit codes such as XFD131 include batteries. Ryobi adds K or K1 for kits, as with the PBLDD01K1, which Ryobi lists with a 2.0Ah battery and charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are new to a platform, a kit is nearly always the better value, because the batteries and charger in a kit cost less than buying them separately. Look at the battery size in the kit. Small 1.5 to 2.0Ah packs keep the tool light and are fine for household jobs. A 4.0 to 5.0Ah pack is worth the extra weight if you plan to use hole saws, drill a lot of holes in one session, or share batteries with a circular saw. Two batteries beat one, because you can charge one while you work with the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own batteries, buy the bare tool. It is the cheapest way to get a new drill, and there is no point paying for another charger. Check compatibility carefully: Milwaukee M12 and M18 batteries are different and do not interchange, and the same is true across every brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kit contents also change by retailer and by season, which is why we do not quote them for every pick. On Amazon, read the listing title and the What's in the Box section, not just the product photos, which sometimes show batteries that are not included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="12v-or-18v" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. 12V or 18V and 20V: Which Size Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milwaukee, DeWalt, Makita and Bosch all sell 12V lines alongside their 18V tools. DeWalt's 20V MAX is the same class as everyone else's 18V; it is the same lithium-ion cell count with a different way of quoting voltage. So the real choice is between a compact 12V drill and a full 18V or 20V drill.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 12V drill like the Milwaukee M12 FUEL 3403-20 is smaller, lighter and easier to carry all day. Milwaukee lists it at 5.9 inches long and 2.5 lb, and it still has a full 1/2-inch chuck. That makes it ideal for cabinet installation, electrical work, furniture assembly and jobs on a ladder. Its limits show on big bits and long sessions: a 1,550 RPM top speed and small batteries mean slower progress through thick lumber.
          </p>

          <p>
            An 18V or 20V drill has more speed, more torque and bigger batteries. It is the right choice for deck building, framing, running hole saws for plumbing and electrical, and anyone who wants one drill for every job. The compact 18V models, like the DCD800 and GSR18V-535CN, close much of the size gap, which is why they are such good general picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our advice: if you only buy one drill, make it 18V or 20V. If you already own an 18V drill and find yourself reaching for something smaller, a 12V drill is a pleasant second tool, and many professionals carry one daily.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="match-the-job" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Matching the Drill to the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Different jobs stress different parts of a drill. For hanging shelves, curtain rods and TV mounts into studs, almost any drill here is enough; the clutch and a good set of bits matter more than motor power. If you are mounting into brick or block, use a hammer drill like the XPH14 with a carbide masonry bit, and run it in low gear with steady pressure rather than leaning on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For deck and fence building, the drill's job is mostly pilot holes and countersinks, while an impact driver should handle the hundreds of screws. A full-size 18V drill like the 2903-20 is the best partner for that work because it will also run the larger bits used for post hardware and through-bolts. For cabinet and furniture work, prioritize light weight, a fine clutch and a compact head: the DCD800, XFD131, 3403-20 and Ryobi are all good fits.
          </p>

          <p>
            For plumbing and electrical rough-in, where you bore large holes through studs and joists with self-feed bits or hole saws, buy the most powerful drill you can and use the side handle if one is supplied. A drill can kick hard when a big bit binds, so keep the drill in low gear and brace your body. Whatever you buy, respect the reaction torque: let go of the trigger the instant a bit binds.
          </p>

          <p>
            For metal, slow down. Low gear, a sharp cobalt or titanium-coated bit and a drop of cutting oil will outlast and outperform high speed every time. Whatever the material, the bit matters as much as the drill, and cheap bits are the most common reason a good drill seems weak. Keep a set of quality hex-shank bits and a torque-sensible clutch setting, and even the compact picks will feel capable. For hand-tool backups, our <a href="/tekton-tools-review/">Tekton tools review</a> covers budget sockets and drivers that pair well with a drill kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Cordless Drill Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already own batteries from one brand, the decision is simple: buy that brand's drill from this list. The Milwaukee 2903-20 or 3403-20 for M18 or M12 owners, the DCD800 for DeWalt 20V MAX, the XFD131 or XPH14 for Makita LXT, the PBLDD01 for Ryobi ONE+, and the GSR18V-535CN for Bosch 18V.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are starting from nothing and want a long-term platform, choose between Milwaukee M18 and DeWalt 20V MAX using our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> guide, then buy the 2903-20 or a DCD800 kit. If you want the most tools for the least money and do mostly home projects, start with the Ryobi PBLDD01 kit. If you work in tight spaces all day, add or start with the M12 FUEL 3403-20. If you need to drill masonry more than once or twice a year, make your drill the XPH14 hammer driver-drill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about the second tool now. A matching impact driver makes almost every building job faster, and our <a href="/best-impact-driver/">best impact driver</a> guide picks one for each of these platforms so the batteries you buy today will run both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing torque numbers across brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each maker measures torque its own way, and DeWalt mostly publishes Unit Watts Out instead. A higher in-lbs figure from one brand does not prove that drill is stronger than another brand's. Compare torque within one brand, and use speed, size, weight and the platform to compare across brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a bare tool by accident</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Milwaukee -20, DeWalt B and Makita Z model numbers are bare tools with no battery or charger. Amazon listings sometimes show batteries in the photos anyway. Read the title and the box contents before you order, especially if this is your first tool on that platform.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Splitting your first tools across brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Batteries are the expensive part, and they only fit one brand. Buying a drill from one company and an impact driver from another means two sets of batteries and two chargers. Choose a platform first, then buy every early tool on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a drill/driver on masonry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A regular drill/driver has no hammer action and will glaze a masonry bit without making much progress in brick or block. Use a hammer drill like the Makita XPH14 or DeWalt DCD805 for occasional masonry, and a rented SDS rotary hammer for serious concrete work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running big bits in high gear</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hole saws, spade bits and self-feed bits need low gear, where the drill multiplies its torque. High gear on a big bit causes stalls, overheated batteries and sudden kickback. Switch down, brace yourself, and let the bit do the cutting.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless drill for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, a compact brushless 18V or 20V drill/driver is the best choice. The DeWalt DCD800 is our pick for home use because it is light, compact and on a huge platform. If budget matters more, the Ryobi PBLDD01 gives you a brushless motor and Ryobi's wide ONE+ range for less money, and it handles typical home projects easily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Milwaukee or DeWalt better for a cordless drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both make excellent drills, and the difference between their flagship drill/drivers is smaller than the difference between their battery platforms. Milwaukee has the deeper compact M12 line; DeWalt has broad availability and many kit bundles. Our Milwaukee vs DeWalt guide compares batteries, warranty and tool range so you can choose the platform before the drill.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a hammer drill or a regular drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You only need a hammer drill if you drill into brick, block, mortar or concrete. For wood, metal, drywall and plastic, a regular drill/driver is lighter and works just as well. A hammer drill can do both, so it is a good single drill if you anchor into masonry a few times a year, but it is heavier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 12V drill strong enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For furniture, cabinets, electrical work, hanging fixtures and most household repairs, yes. A good 12V drill like the Milwaukee M12 FUEL 3403-20 is rated at 400 in-lbs and keeps a 1/2-inch chuck. It is slower with large spade bits and hole saws and its small batteries drain faster, so choose 18V or 20V if you build decks or bore large holes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does UWO mean on a DeWalt drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                UWO stands for Unit Watts Out, a power rating DeWalt uses instead of a torque figure on many of its drills. It combines speed and torque into one number and is useful for comparing DeWalt drills with each other. It cannot be compared directly with the in-lbs torque figures that Milwaukee, Makita, Ryobi and Bosch publish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much torque do I need in a cordless drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less than the marketing suggests. Driving screws and drilling holes up to about 1 inch in wood needs only a modest amount, which every drill on this list provides. Torque matters for large hole saws, self-feed bits and auger bits. Because brands rate torque differently, compare figures only within one brand, and buy a full-size 18V drill if you regularly use large bits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a drill and impact driver combo kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, if you plan to do any building, decking or furniture assembly. Combo kits on one platform often cost only a little more than the drill kit alone and include two batteries and a charger. The impact driver handles long screws and lag bolts that would strain a drill, while the drill stays set up for holes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

